Short answer: not strictly into a `_test.go`, but yes with a build tag — which gets you the same thing (test-only, can't ship by accident).

The constraint: `_test.go` files are excluded by `go build`. The integration test runs `entire login` as a subprocess that's built via plain `go build` in `setup_test.go:25`, so anything in a `_test.go` wouldn't be in the subprocess binary, and the env-var hook would silently no-op there. That's exactly the case we need it for.

The clean equivalent is a build tag — e.g. `//go:build integrationtest` — on a dedicated `store_testfile.go`, plus passing `-tags=integrationtest` from `setup_test.go`'s `go build`. Production releases (no tag) physically don't contain the file-backend code or the env-var read, so nothing ships.

Sketch:

```go
// store.go  (always compiled — no env var read here)
type Store struct {
    service string
    backend tokenBackend     // unexported interface
}

type tokenBackend interface {
    save(service, key, val string) error
    get(service, key string) (string, error)
    delete(service, key string) error
}

func NewStore() *Store {
    return &Store{service: keyringService, backend: chooseBackend()}
}

// Default; overridden by init() in the tagged file.
var chooseBackend = func() tokenBackend { return keyringBackend{} }

type keyringBackend struct{}
// keyringBackend.save/get/delete wrap keyring.Set/Get/Delete
```

```go
// store_testfile.go
//go:build integrationtest

package auth

const testAuthStoreFileEnv = "ENTIRE_TEST_AUTH_STORE_FILE"

func init() {
    chooseBackend = func() tokenBackend {
        if p := os.Getenv(testAuthStoreFileEnv); p != "" {
            return &fileBackend{path: p}
        }
        return keyringBackend{}
    }
}

type fileBackend struct{ path string }
// fileBackend.save/get/delete + readTokenFile/writeTokenFile live here
```

Then in `cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/setup_test.go`:

```go
buildCmd := exec.Command("go", "build", "-tags=integrationtest", "-o", testBinaryPath, ".")
```

And the existing in-process unit test (`TestNewStore_UsesTestStoreFile`) moves to a `store_testfile_test.go` with the same `//go:build integrationtest` tag, so it only runs when the tag is on. `mise run test:integration` already passes `-tags=integration`; we'd extend it to `-tags=integration,integrationtest` (or just reuse the `integration` tag — debatable, but `integration` is currently used to gate a different package's tests, so a separate name is cleaner).

This gives you:
- Production `go build` → keyring only, no env var, no JSON read/write code in the binary at all.
- Integration build → file backend available, gated by env var.
- The "clearly test-only" property you want, enforced by the compiler.

Want me to do the refactor on this branch, or write it up as a follow-up issue/PR?

